# project-setup.local.sh.example - the per-user project-setup seam.
#
# Copy this file to .devcontainer/project-setup.local.sh and edit it:
#
#   cp .devcontainer/project-setup.local.sh.example .devcontainer/project-setup.local.sh
#
# The live file is gitignored (see .gitignore's private-files block): it
# carries per-developer overrides only, never a committed value. The
# postcreate sources it, as the container user and in the same environment
# context as project-setup.sh (WORK_DIR and shell.env exported, workspace on
# PATH), AFTER the project script and AFTER the postcreate resolved the
# opencode config -- so everything here overrides provisioning.
#
# The same three rules that govern shell.env apply here: no committed values,
# no secrets (a credential belongs in the hostcreds manifest; see below),
# and anything you leave commented stays untouched.

########################
# Model and default agent
########################
# The postcreate resolves the provider toggles (ZAI_ENABLED /
# OPENROUTER_ENABLED, from devcontainer.json's containerEnv) into
# enabled_providers and the model matrix. Override the result here, for this
# user only. Every value must name a generated agent or a provider/model
# pair the installed config defines; the generated set is listed in
# ~/.config/opencode/lib/generated-agents.json.
#
# Pin the session driver to the openrouter flagship:
#   jq '.model = "openrouter/glm-5.3" | .small_model = "openrouter/glm-5.3-flash"' \
#     ~/.config/opencode/opencode.json > /tmp/oc.json \
#     && mv /tmp/oc.json ~/.config/opencode/opencode.json
#
# Make a generated build driver the default agent:
#   jq '.default_agent = "build-zai-max-glm-5.3-flash"' \
#     ~/.config/opencode/opencode.json > /tmp/oc.json \
#     && mv /tmp/oc.json ~/.config/opencode/opencode.json

########################
# MCP toggles
########################
# Disable one MCP server for this user only:
#   jq '.mcp.playwright.enabled = false' \
#     ~/.config/opencode/opencode.json > /tmp/oc.json \
#     && mv /tmp/oc.json ~/.config/opencode/opencode.json

########################
# Provider API keys
########################
# Do NOT export a provider key here. opencode reads keys only through the
# {env:ZAI_API_KEY} / {env:OPENROUTER_API_KEY} interpolations the shipped
# config carries, and the values are exported automatically by the hostcreds
# startup block -- the manifest (gitignored,
# .devcontainer/hostcreds.map.json, created by 'make init' from its committed
# example) names the entry, 'make creds-init' stores it in the keychain, and
# 'make push-creds' delivers it into the container:
#
#   OPENROUTER_API_KEY -> add to .devcontainer/hostcreds.map.json:
#     "OPENROUTER_API_KEY": { "source": "keychain" }
#   then: make creds-init CREDS_INIT_ARGS='--stdin OPENROUTER_API_KEY'
#         make push-creds
#
# (ZAI_API_KEY follows the same shape; it is already the entry this
# repository's committed configuration expects.) Nothing here is needed for
# that flow -- the block below exists only if you must re-read an exported
# key for a non-opencode consumer in this same environment:
#
# : "${OPENROUTER_API_KEY:?OPENROUTER_API_KEY is not exported; add it to the hostcreds manifest and run make creds-init + make push-creds}"

########################
# gws state restore
########################
# The gws CLI keeps its OAuth refresh token (encrypted) in ~/.config/gws; a
# fresh container starts without it and the postcreate notices. Restore it
# from a backup you keep outside this repository:
#
#   mkdir -p ~/.config/gws
#   cp /path/to/your/gws-state-backup/* ~/.config/gws/
